fuxi: 0104 -- read clustering: per-slot read-ahead window (ufsra, 256 KiB)

The driver serves each strategy call with its own SCSI read, and the VFS/HFS+
layer issues ~4 KiB per call, so the 1 MiB bounce buffer is never filled: a cold
boot of the 3.3 GB dyld shared cache is order 800k separate 4 KiB commands, each
one a doorbell, a poll and a completion.  0103 makes those overlap across eight
slots; it does not reduce the count.  This does.

On a read smaller than RA_SMALL_READ (64 KiB) the slot fetches an ALIGNED window
(RA_DEFAULT_KIB = 256 KiB, i.e. 64 device blocks) with ONE command into its own
bounce buffer and serves the requested range out of it.  The next sequential
4 KiB reads hit the window and are a memcpy with no command at all.  Measured
against the request size that is 64x fewer reads; a 64 KiB-granularity workload
still sees 4x.  Boot argument ufsra=<KiB> sets the size (clamped to the 1 MiB
bounce buffer, rounded up to RA_MIN_KIB = 8); ufsra=0 disables the window
entirely and restores the pre-0104 one-command-per-request path, which is the
A/B baseline on the same kernel.

Correctness rules, in order of how badly they fail:
  * the window is PER SLOT and lives in that slot's bounce buffer.  A window
    shared between slots would be the same single-bounce data-corruption bug
    0103 removed.
  * every command that moves data through a slot's bounce buffer clears that
    slot's window first (ufs_scsi_slot, len != 0): the buffer IS the window.
  * every completed write and every SYNCHRONIZE CACHE bumps su.write_seq, and a
    window is only served while slot->win_seq still matches it.  That
    invalidates the windows of ALL slots without walking the array -- a write
    through slot B must not leave slot A serving a pre-write copy of the same
    blocks, which is exactly how a filesystem gets corrupted.
  * the window start is aligned to its own size in device-block space and then
    CLAMPED to su.first_block .. su.first_block + su.nblocks - 1, and its length
    is clamped to the partition end.  A window can therefore never name a block
    outside the partition, and a read past the partition end still returns
    EINVAL from the unchanged bounds check in fuxi_ufs_strategy.
  * a window is used only when it FULLY contains the requested blocks, and the
    fill checks containment against the CLAMPED start/length rather than only
    the size -- a window pinned to the partition base can have enough blocks and
    still end before the request does, which would have served the wrong blocks.
    On a partial overlap or a miss the old window is dropped and one aligned
    window covering the whole request is fetched; this never serves part of a
    request from an old window plus part from a new one, so a misaligned request
    cannot return a mixed/stale range.  A request the window cannot contain
    (64 KiB <= request, or a window clamped smaller than the request) falls back
    to the plain per-request read.
  * writes, the GPT watch and the log flusher do not use the window at all:
    the GPT watch reads absolute block 1, which is below su.first_block, so
    slot_window_fill refuses it (ENOENT) and it takes the plain path.

No change to the write path, the partition bounds, the 'lba < 6' guard or the
GPT fence: the only fencing code touched is the addition of the write_seq bump
after a write and after a cache flush.

Strings that prove this patch applied, for the artifact check:
'read-ahead window %u KiB (%u blocks), ufsra=0 disables' and 'ufsra'.
